    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d13">

                <pb facs="0115" n="89" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76v26/pg89"/>

                <head>13. Letter From President Nixon to Egyptian President Sadat<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d13-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1974-01-17">January 17,
                        1974</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <opener>
                    <salute>Dear Mr. President:</salute>
                </opener>
                <p>I understand that once the agreement between Egypt and Israel on disengagement of
                    forces is in effect, you intend to begin work looking toward an early return of
                    the Suez Canal to full operation and toward the rehabilitation of the cities and
                    towns along the Canal and the resumption of normal economic activities in that
                    area. I want you to know that if you proceed in this way the United States gives
                    you its assurance that Israel will refrain from taking any military action
                    against those civilian centers, installations and populations.</p>
                <p>Sincerely,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed corresp="#p_NRM_1">Richard Nixon</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d13-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d13-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, Nixon Presidential Materials, NSC Files, Kissinger
                    Office Files, Box 133, Country Files, Middle East, Egypt, Volume 10, February
                    1974. Secret. According to Kissinger’s memoirs, Kissinger drafted the letter
                    himself. (<hi rendition="#italic">Years of Upheaval</hi>, pp. 834–835)</p>
            </note>
